.SH NAME
composed_component \- See the module
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ComposedComponent::new(state: S, on_change: impl Fn(S) \-> M + 'a)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
See the module.
.PP
\fBComposedComponent\fR: the opposite of \fBMetaComponent\fR. A meta component has one piece of content and chooses between presentations. A composed component has several components and \fBone shared state\fR, and renders as a single widget.
.PP
Every part is built from the shared state and answers with the next state. Whichever part the user touches, the application gets one value through one \fBon_change\fR, and every part is built from that value on the next view, so they cannot disagree. A histogram over a range slider, a color box next to a hex entry, a calendar beside a date entry: each pair edits one value.
.PP
.nf
.RS
ComposedComponent::new(self.range, Msg::Range)
    .part(|r| {
        BarChart::histogram(&self.histogram)
            .selected(|i| self.histogram.selected(i, *r))   // the bars inside the range
            .on_pick(|i| self.histogram.bin_range(i))        // a pressed bar is a range
            .into()
    })
    .part(|r| range_slider(0.0..=100.0, *r, |moved| moved).into())
    .normalize(|(lo, hi)| (lo.min(hi), lo.max(hi)))
.RE
.fi
.IP \(bu 2
\fBState.\fR The application owns it (controlled); \fBnew\fR takes the current value and \fBon_change\fR the message that brings back the next.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBParts.\fR \fBpart\fR gets the current state and returns an element whose messages are the *next state*. Add as many as needed, in display order.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRule.\fR \fBnormalize\fR runs on every next state before it is reported, so the invariant (ordering, clamping) lives in one place, not in each part.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLayout.\fR A column by default; \fBaxis\fR for a row.
